Z||000000||ka4nkf||11-22-2001||02:35 AM||||Why does brake fluid get dirty in the reservoir? It has no return line so what makes it get dirty. I take a syringe about once a year and suck it all out and fill back up with new fluid.<p>[This message has been edited by ka4nkf (edited 11-22-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Standard||11-22-2001||03:20 AM||||I would think mainly the rubber brake lines wearing out over the years.  Also any water that gets in there may start rusting the metal lines, and that'll flake off and make the fluid dirty.  The only way to get it completly clean again is to drain, flush, and refill the whole system.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wrench||11-22-2001||09:14 AM||||If you are having to top off the reservoir from time to time you may have a vary small leak in the system. ad a similar situation with my clutch master cylinder. Clutch worked fine but fluid looked like crap. Had to top it off once a month. A detailed inspection found a small leak on he clutch master cylinder around the "banjo push rod". Decided to replace the master cylinder and wish I didn't! Had the typical "bleeding problems". Finally broke down a made a "homebuilt pressure bleeder". Worked great. I'll post details if I can figure how to post the pictures. Just my 2 cents. I have an 86 GT, Nitros equipped, 190,000 miles and still running strong!! ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jake_Dragon||11-22-2001||11:39 AM||||Posted for wrench in no particular order<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-001f_7.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-003f_11.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mvc-004f_6.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||11-22-2001||01:38 PM||||I like it!  Have you had any problems with aeration?  Maybe not -- I suppose it operates under fairly low pressure.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||11-22-2001||02:51 PM||||Wear, corrosion, etc, will discolor brake fluid and leave it with suspended particles.<P>As pointed out above, the only way to remove that trash is flushing. When you just change what is in the tank, the stuff in the rest of the system eventually works back into it. <P>The only way to remove 100% of the trash is to tear down the calipers and so on but that's not really required. Likewise you don't want to drain the system unless you are already planning to work on the MC.<P>There are a bunch of bleeding/flushing tips and fluid info in my cave. You can make a power bleeder like above or use a vacuum bleed set. The one to avoid, especially for flushing, is pedal bleeding. That method can damage the MC in cars with really trashed up systems. (Trash and corrosion build up in the area of the MC that isn't used durring normal braking. When you full stroke the MC, you hit that trash and damage seals or jam the secondary piston.)<P>The power bleeders do run at low pressure. Even the comercial ones are usually regulated down to 4-6PSI to prevent damaging plastic MC tanks. Even that low pressure can make a giant mess really easy. make sure the drain hose is firmly held to the bleeder and that the other end can't come out of the catch bottle. <P>As you know, brake fluid is nasty stuff. Chemist type safety goggles or a full face shield is a must when using a power bleeder. Chemist type goggles have 4-6 small splash resistant vent plugs instead of perforated sides like regular safety goggles you'd use for wood/metal work.<P>There are a bunch of ways to make a power bleeder. I saw one once that simply screwed onto the fluid bottle. It used a little bicycle pump and a plastic "vacuum ball" as a tank to build pressure. It was pretty neat. The guy stored it in a standard tool box as part of his brake tools set. sure made cleanup a snap. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
